## 1. Introduction
The energy crisis has stimulated intense exploration into renewable and environmentally-friendly energy sources [1]. In order to efficiently utilize these new sustainable energy sources, electrochemical energy storages have been developed because of its high energy efficiency and clean storage systems [2–7]. Among the electrochemical energy storage devices, supercapacitors have attracted much interest due to their higher power density, fast charge-discharge time and long cycle life [8,9]. According to the energy storage mechanism, supercapacitors can be generally classified into electric double layer capacitance and pseudocapacitance [10]. In general, the specific capacitance of pseudocapacitive materials is higher than that of the electric double layer materials, owing to reversible redox reactions between the electrolyte and electrode [11]. Therefore, the development of the suitable pseudocapacitive materials is very important for improving the energy density of the supercapacitors.
Various pseudocapacitive materials, such as transition metal hydroxides and metal oxides [12,13], conducting polymers [14] and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [15,16] have been developed for supercapacitors. Among them, MOF is a new kind of porous materials, which has been widely used in gas separation, catalysis and storage fields [17]. The direct use of MOFs as supercapacitor electrode materials has been investigated in recently years owning to their remarkable surface area, tunable pore structure and rich redox reaction sites. For instance, Han et al. [18] successfully synthesized a cobalt based MOF by solvothermal method, exhibiting a specific capacitance of \(179.2~\mathrm{Fg}^{-1}\) at the scan rate of \(10\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). The UiO-66 prepared by Tang et al. [19] also exhibited the good electrochemical performance. However, the direct use of MOFs as pseudocapacitive materials is still a challenge due to the poor electrical conductivity of most MOFs during the electrochemical charge-discharge process.
One of the efficient ways to overcome the disadvantage to further increase the capacitance is to tailor design of MOFs with the unique structure [20–22]. Numerous efforts have been devoted to design the well-controlled structured MOFs for improving the electrochemical performance. For instance, Du et al. [23] synthesized a layered structure of Ni-MOF by adjusting the ratio of BTC and nickel ions, which exhibited a good specific capacitance of \(1057\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at current density of \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Xu et al. [24] synthesized a material of 1 D Ni-MOF derived nanorods using salicylate ions as organic ligands, the prepared Ni-MOF showed a high specific capacitance of \(1698\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) due to unique 1D nanostructure. Modifying the surface property of MOFs is also a vitally important strategy to further improve the electrochemical performance. Presently, the surface properties of MOFs are mainly regulated by the incorporation of another different central metal ion into the same framework or the introduction of functional groups onto the organic ligands [25,26]. For example, He et al. [27] found that the introduction of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) can effectively enhance the conductivity of the Ni-based MOFs, the obtained Ni/Co-MOF has better electrochemical performance than the Ni-MOF. The Wei et al. [28] reported that the electrochemical performance of Ni-MOF can be significantly improved by doping with Zn ion. The Zn-doped Ni-MOF can deliver a high specific capacitance of \(854\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) when explored at the current density of \(10\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). However, the current design strategy to synthesize MOFs with controllable
structure and surface properties is mainly based on the rational selection of metal ions/organic ligands and the optimization of reaction conditions. The studies on the mixed-ligand effect on the structure and surface properties of MOFs are rarely reported. The incorporation of another different organic ligand may lead to better redox behavior of MOFs. Thus exploitation of facile mixed-ligand synthetic method for engineering the structure and surface properties of MOFs is very important to improve the electrochemical performance.
Herein, we report a mixed-ligand approach to control the structure and surface properties of Ni-MOF to increase the pseudocapacitance. BTC has been used as the modulator to partially replace the terephthalic acid (PTA) ligands in the Ni-MOF. Both BTC and PTA are the carboxylated ligands which can connect Ni ions through coordination bonds. The BTC can influence the morphology of the Ni-MOF via competitive interaction with PTA ligands. The similar molecular sizes of BTC and PTA ensure that the introduction of BTC could not destroy the crystalline of Ni-MOF. After introducing the BTC ligands, the morphology of Ni-MOF changes from aggregated nanosheets to nanosheets with inserted albizia flower-like spheres (albizia flower-like spheres@nanosheets). The albizia flower-like hierarchical nanostructure has been proven that it can provide much more redox reaction sites and a short pathway for transport/diffusion of electrolyte ions between the electrode and electrolyte interface [29,30]. The increased basal plane spacing between the nanosheet layers caused by the inserted albizia flower-like spheres may offer buffer strains during the charge-discharge process [31]. The mixed organic ligands components also may regulate the surface properties of the Ni-MOF, the adsorption simulation shows that the adsorption energy of \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\) on the mixed organic ligands is increased after introducing the BTC ligands. Therefore, owing to the synergic effects of unique structure and mixed ligand composition, the modified Ni-MOF shows a high specific capacitance and an excellent rate capability. The asymmetric supercapacitor based on the modified Ni-MOF also achieves the good electrochemical performance.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Fabrication of hierarchical porous metal-organic framework (Ni-MOF)
In a typical procedure, 3 mmol \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and 2 mmol organic ligands (the mole ratio of PTA: BTC was set to 10:0, 9:1, 8:2, and 7:3) were dissolved in \(35\;\mathrm{mL}\) DMF using magnetic stirring at room temperature. After \(10\;\mathrm{min}\), the mixture was then transformed into a \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave and heated to \(160\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\;\mathrm{h}\) with the heating rate of about \(5\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{min}^{-1}\). After cooling to room temperature, the products were centrifuged and washed with DMF and ethanol several times. After drying in vacuum over \(12\;\mathrm{h}\), the final Ni-MOFs (marked as Ni-MOF-0, Ni-MOF-1, Ni-MOF-2, Ni-MOF-3) were obtained.
## 2.2. Material characterization
The field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, FEI, Nova NanoSEM 450) and transmission electron microscope (FEI Ti-Imai G2 F30 system) were used to investigate the morphologies
and structures of the samples. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were examined by using a Shimadzu X-ray diffractometer. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Mettler-Toledo) was used to investigate the thermostability of the samples. Nitrogen sorption isotherms were measured at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) on a physical sorption instrument (Autosorb-iQ-C). Surface chemistry properties of the samples were obtained from the high resolution X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB250Xi).
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
To prepare the working electrodes, the as-made samples, binder poly (tetrafluoroethylene) and carbon black with the mass ratio of 7:1:2 were mixed together by adding a small amount of ethanol, after drying at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the mixture was rolled into carbon film and then pressed onto the nickel foam. For the three-electrode test, Pt foil was used as the counter electrode and \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) electrode was used as the reference electrode. The electrochemical performances of the samples were evaluated by the CHI 760E electrochemistry workstation.
## 2.4. Fabrication of the asymmetric supercapacitor cell (ASC)
The ASC was assembled by using Ni-MOF-2 as the positive electrode and activated carbon as the negative electrode. The mass ratio between the positive \((\mathfrak{m}^{+})\) and negative \((\mathfrak{m}^{-})\) active material is determined via \(\mathsf{q}^{+}\mathsf{m}^{+}{=}\mathsf{q}^{-}\mathsf{m}^{-}\). where \(\mathsf{q}^{+}\)and \(\mathsf{q}^{-}\)are the gravimetric capacities of electrodes measured in three electrode system. The cyclic voltammetry curves and galvanostatic charge/discharge voltage profiles were recorded in a potential range of \(0{-}1.6\,\mathrm{V}\) using a CHI 760E electrochemistry workstation in 6 M KOH electrolyte. The cycling performance was also conducted on the same electrochemistry workstation. The energy density (E, Wh \(\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\)) and power density (P, W \(\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\)) were calculated on the basis of the total mass of active materials of the two electrodes according to the equations:
\[
\mathsf{E}=\mathsf{CV}^2/2
\]
\[
\mathrm{P=E/t,}
\]
where C is the specific capacitance of the ASC \((\mathsf{F}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1})\), V is the potential (V), t is the discharge time (s).
## 2.5. Simulation details
All Optimize geometry and the adsorption behaviors of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\)on the mixed organic ligands were carried out by Adsorption Locator program using Materials Studio package with Forcefield of Universal. 100,000 loading steps and 3 heating cycles with 15,000 steps per cycle were used in the simulation. For the electrostatic terms, group based summation method was used with cutoff distance of \(12.5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) and buffer width of \(0.5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\). In the van der Waals terms, atom based summation method and cubic spline truncation method were employed with the cutoff distance of \(12.5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\), spline width of \(1\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\), and Buffer width of \(0.5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization of Ni-MOFs
The synthetic procedure of Ni-MOFs is achieved with the existence of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and organic ligands in a one-step hydrothermal method as shown in Fig. 1. In the absence of trimesic acid (BTC), the coordination of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and terephthalic acid (PTA) results in the aggregated nanosheets. Nevertheless, when BTC is introduced, nanosheets with the inserted albizia flower-like spheres are formed. The BTC may play an important role in changing the nanosheets to the albizia flower-like spheres.
The morphologies of the Ni-MOFs observed by the field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) are shown in Figs. 2 and S1. Figs. 2a–b and S1a–b show that in the absence of BTC, Ni-MOF-0 nanosheets aggregate into bulk structures. The morphology change of Ni-MOF-1 with BTC is visible, it can be found that the albizia flower-like spheres with diameter of ca.300–500 nm are formed between the nanosheets (Figs. 2c–d and S1c–d). When increasing mole ratio of the BTC to PTA to 2:8, the albizia flower-like spheres with increased diameter of ca.700–900 nm are observed (Figs. 2e–f and S1e–f). The energy dispersive spectroscopy elemental (EDS) mapping images of Ni-MOF-2 (Fig. S2) reveal the presence and uniform distribution of C, O, and Ni elements throughout the sample. The albizia flower-like spheres may increase the basal plane spacing between the nanosheet layers, which may provide a large contact area between electrode and electrolyte as well as buffer strains during the charge-discharge process. However, when the mole ratio of the BTC to PTA is increased to 3:7, the nanosheets are nearly disappeared, and the aggregate bulk spheres with fluffy nanowires are observed (Figs. 2g–h and S1g–h), indicating that the excess BTC can broke the structure of nanosheets with inserted albizia flower-like spheres.
The morphology of Ni-MOF-2 was also investigated by the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) in Figs. 3 and S3. It can be clearly seen that Ni-MOF-2 is composed of albizia flower-like spheres (red arrows) and nanosheets (blue arrows), which is consistent with the SEM images. The HR-TEM images in the inset of Fig. 3a reveals that the lattice fringe was ca. \(0.52\,\mathrm{nm}\), which is corresponding to the (2 0 0) plane. As shown in Figs. 3b and S3b, the nanowires of the albizia flower-like spheres have a diameter range of ca. \(10{-}20\ \mathrm{nm}\). Such a thin nanowires structure can provide rich redox reaction sites to boost the faradaic reaction, which may improve the electrochemical performance of the Ni-MOFs.
Fig. 4a displays the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of all the MOFs. The diffraction pattern of Ni-MOF-0 is similar with the MOFs synthesized by Ni ions and PTA ligands from other reports [20,32]. The diffraction peaks occurring at \(86^{\circ}\), \(11.8^{\circ}\), \(17.1^{\circ}\), \(20.7^{\circ}\) and \(23.8^{\circ}\) are corresponded to (1 0 0), (0 1 0), (2 0 0), (1 0 1) and (0 2 0), respectively (CCDC no. 638860). After introducing BTC, the crystal structures of all the MOFs are nearly unchanged. It is noted that there are no additional peaks from a crystal solely with BTC and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) appeared in the patterns, indicating that BTC ligands may be incorporated in the parent structure. The FT-IR image of Ni-MOFs
is presented in Fig. 4b, the broad band in the region 3000– \(3600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is assigned to absorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules. The peaks at around \(833~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(742~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for all samples are assigned to the \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) of aromatic deformation vibration. Moreover, the peaks at around \(1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1375~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are attributed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes of the coordinated carboxyl groups, respectively, due to BTC and PTA ligands [15]. All of the FT-IR peaks of the samples are almost the same, confirming that the prepared Ni-MOFs have similar components.
The thermal stability of the Ni-MOF-0 and Ni-MOF-2 were investigated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. As shown in Fig. S4, it can be found the weight losses below \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) are \(4.0\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) and \(7.4\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) for Ni-MOF-0 and Ni-MOF-2, which could be attributed the loss of water molecules. The weight loss in the range of \(150{-}450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can be related to the decomposition of the organic frameworks. It is note that the weight loss of organic ligands in Ni-MOF-2 \((60.9\mathrm{\wt\%})\) is slightly higher than that of Ni
MOF-0 \((58.3\mathrm{\wt\%})\), which may be assigned to the more carboxylic acid groups of introduced BTC.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was further performed to obtain the information of the components of the Ni-MOF-0 and Ni-MOF-2. As shown in Fig. S5, it can be clearly found that the C, O, and Ni elements are the main components of both the Ni-MOF-0 and Ni-MOF-2. For detailed information, the C 1s spectra of Ni-MOF-0 in Fig. 5a can be divided into two individual peaks, referring to \(\mathsf{C{-}C/C{=}C}\) bond \((284.5\,\mathrm{eV})\) and the carboxylate carbon \((288.1\,\mathrm{eV})\) of the organic ligands. For the C 1s spectra of Ni-MOF-2, the peaks of graphite carbon and carbon in COOH are centered at 284.5 and \(288.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) respectively (Fig. 5b). The carboxylate carbon is increased from 28.1 at.% in Ni-MOF-0 to \(29.5\ \mathrm{at.\%}\) in Ni-MOF-2, confirming the successful introduction of the BTC in Ni-MOF-2. The result is also consistent with the TGA data. The high-resolution XPS spectra of Ni \(2\mathrm{p}\) for Ni-MOF-0 was fitted into two major peaks at 873.7 and \(856.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) with a spin-energy separation of \(17.7\,\mathrm{eV}\), which indicates
the existence of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\). Moreover, the two other peaks centered at about 879.8 and \(861.5\;\mathrm{eV}\) can be assigned to the shakeup satellites. Similarly, this is also the case for Ni 2p XPS spectra of Ni-MOF-2 (Fig. 5d). The \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) specie will play an important role for the high pseudocapacitance of electrode materials [33]. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller surface area of Ni-MOF-2 (Fig. S6) is \(64.8\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\), which is higher than that of some metal oxides and metal hydroxides reported elsewhere [34–36], this may be helpful for Ni-MOF-2 to achieve the good capacitive performance.
It is well known that the pseudocapacitive behaviour of Ni-based compound may come from the intercalation and deintercalation of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) during electrochemical reactions [37,38]. Adsorption simulations (Fig. 6) clearly show that the incorporation of BTC facilitates the adsorption of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ions on the mixed organic ligands compared to that of a single type of PTA-ligand, indicating that the surface property of Ni-MOF could be changed and the reversible redox reaction kinetics of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) may be improved. Specifically, \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ions could adsorb on mixed organic ligands surface (mole ratio of the BTC to PTA is 2:8) with the strongest adsorption energy \((10.06\,\mathrm{eV})\) than those of others, suggesting that the Ni-MOF-2 may exhibit excellent electrochemical performance.
## 3.2. Electrochemical performance of Ni-MOFs
The electrochemical performance of the Ni-MOFs electrodes was first tested in a three-electrode cell in 6 M KOH aqueous elec
trolyte. At the scan rate of \(5\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), Fig. 7a shows that all cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves exhibit pairs of broad redox couple peaks. It can be found that Ni-MOF-2 electrode exhibits larger CV areas compared to those of other Ni-MOFs, indicating its higher specific capacitance. When increasing the scan rate to \(50\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Fig. 7b), the increased intense redox peaks can be observed in all the CV curves of Ni-MOFs. The galvanostatic charge/discharge (GC) curves of the samples at a current density of \(1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) are shown in Fig. 7c. It can be found that all the potential time responses of Ni-MOFs are nonlinear, indicating that the capacitances mainly originate from the faradaic behavior, which is consistent with the results of CV curves. The specific capacitances of the samples calculated from the results of GC curves are shown in Fig. 7d. Ni-MOF-0 shows a specific capacitance of \(721\,\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}\) at a current density of \(1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and delivers \(209.0\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at a high current density of \(20\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with the capacitance retention of only \(29\%\). Nevertheless, all the Ni-MOFs with BTC ligands show the increased specific capacitance, which can reach to \(838\,\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}\) (Ni-MOF-1), \(920\,\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}\) (Ni-MOF-2) and \(775\,\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}\) (Ni-MOF-3), respectively. The electrochemical performance of Ni-MOFs is comparable to some other reports of MOF-based electrodes (Table S1 [22,23,27,39–43]. The charge and discharge process of the prepared Ni-MOFs might be represented by the following equations [21,26]:
In addition, compared with capacitance retention of Ni-MOF-0, the other Ni-MOFs show better rate capability of \(54\%\) (Ni-MOF-1), \(61\%\) (Ni-MOF-2) and \(52\%\) (Ni-MOF-3) at high current density of 20 \(\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) , respectively, indicating the improved redox reaction kinetics in the modified Ni-MOFs. The charge transport characteristics of the electrodes were measured by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). The Nyquist plots and equivalent circuit fitting are shown in Fig. 7e. It can be observed that the diameters of semicircle in the high-frequency range of Ni-MOFs with BTC ligands are smaller than that of Ni-MOF-0, indicating the low charge-transfer resistances. For the equivalent circuit, \(\mathrm{R_{s}}\) is ohmic resistance of the system, \(C_{\mathrm{d}}\) correspond to the double layer capacity, \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) is on behalf of charge transfer resistance and \(\mathrm{Q_{A}}\) is according to the
adsorption of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) onto electrode surfaces. The values of these elements are summarized in Table S2. Among these, Ni-MOF-2 has lowest charge-transfer resistance (1.376 O) and ohmic resistance of the system (0.7069 O), which is beneficial for the fast ion transport. The cycle stability is also characterized at the current density of \(5\;\mathsf{A}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) , the results are shown in Fig. 7f. Ni-MOF-0 possesses a capacitance retention \(61\%\) after 3000 charge-discharge cycles. On the other hand, the capacitance retention of Ni-MOF-2 \((80\%)\) is higher than that of Ni-MOF-0, indicate the improved cycling performance. The best electrochemical performance of Ni-MOF-2 may be due to the unique nanosheets with the inserted albizia flower-like spheres structure and the mixed ligand composition. Firstly, the nanowires on the albizia flower-like spheres and the 2D nanosheets can provide rich redox reaction sites. Moreover, the suitably sized "spacer" albizia flower-like spheres can hinder the aggregation of the 2D nanosheets to provide fast transport pathways to reduce the charge-transfer resistance. In addition, the optimized amount of the added BTC can increase the adsorption energy of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ions which may improve the reversible redox reaction kinetics in the electrode materials.
The asymmetric supercapacitor has been constructed with Ni-MOF-2 as the positive electrode, and commercial active carbon (AC) as the negative electrode to further evaluate the real applications of the as-synthesized Ni-MOF-2 electrode. As shown in Fig. S7, the potential window of Ni-MOF-2 electrode is in the voltage range of \(0{-}0.6\ \mathrm{V}\) while that of AC electrode is in the voltage range of \(-1.0\) to \(_{0\mathrm{~V~}}\) at a scan rate of \(10\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) in 6 M KOH as the electrolyte. Therefore, the voltage range of \(0{-}1.6\,\mathrm{V}\) can be chosen as the operated electrochemical window. Both the CV and GC curves of the asymmetric supercapacitor cell (ASC) presented in Fig. 8a–b suggest that the capacitance of ASC is resulted from the pseudocapacitance and electric double-layer capacitance. The
specific capacitances calculated from GC curves at different current densities are shown in Fig. 8c, Ni-MOF-2//AC yields a high specific capacitance of ca. \(119\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at the current density of \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and \(56\%\) capacitance retention can be obtained at a high current density of \(10\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The Ragone plot (Fig. 8d) shows that Ni-MOF-2//AC cell can achieve a high energy density of \(42.4\;\mathrm{Wh}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at a power density of \(0.8\;\mathrm{kW}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\), and the energy density can still keep \(23.7\;\mathrm{Wh}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) even at a power density of \(8.0\;\mathrm{kW}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). Meanwhile, the cycling performance of the Ni-MOF-2//AC asymmetric supercapacitor was also evaluated at the current density of \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). After continuous galvanostatic charge-discharge, the Ni-MOF-2//AC can retain ca. \(75\%\) of the initial specific capacitance after 3000 cycles (Fig. 8e), indicating good cycling stability.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a Ni-based albizia flower-like spheres@nanosheets structured MOF has been prepared by a mixed-ligand approach. BTC has been used as the modulator to partially
replace the PTA in the Ni-MOF and incorporated in the parent structure of the Ni-MOF. The introduction of BTC can induce the formation of the albizia flower-like spheres@nanosheets structure and facilitate adsorption of \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)ions on the surface. Benefiting from synergistic effect of the surface property and unique structure, the prepared Ni-MOF-2 electrode exhibits excellent performance of \(920\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at a current density of \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and good rate capability of \(562\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(20\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Moreover, an ASC cell based on Ni-MOF-2 positive electrode and an AC negative electrode delivers a high energy density of \(42.4\;\mathrm{Wh}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at power density of \(0.8\;\mathrm{kW}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). This work may provide an innovative strategy to tune the structure and surface properties of MOFs for high-performance supercapacitors.
